To implement these changes, we need to update the Autocomplete components to render the descriptive text when an option is selected and add a dedicated "MEMO" indicator for CGL.
### 1. Update Branch Autocomplete
We will add a helper Typography block below the Autocomplete to display the description of the selected branch.
**Replace your existing Branch Autocomplete (around line 621) with this:**
```javascript
{/* Branch */}
<Grid item size={{ xl: 1.5, lg: 2 }}>
  <Autocomplete
    autoHighlight
    popupIcon={null}
    options={branchSearch.options}
    getOptionLabel={(option) => option?.code ? String(option.code) : ""}
    renderOption={(props, option) => (
      <li {...props} key={option.code}>
        <Typography noWrap>{option.code} - {option.name}</Typography>
      </li>
    )}
    value={row.branch || null}
    onChange={(event, newValue) => handleRowChange(row.id, "branch", newValue)}
    inputValue={branchInputValues[row.id] || ""}
    onInputChange={(event, newInputValue, reason) => {
        if (reason === 'input') {
            const cleanInput = newInputValue.replace(BRANCH_REPLACE_REGEX, "").substring(0, 5);
            setBranchInputValues(prev => ({ ...prev, [row.id]: cleanInput }));
            searchBranches(cleanInput, row.id);
        } else {
            setBranchInputValues(prev => ({ ...prev, [row.id]: newInputValue }));
        }
    }}
    renderInput={(params) => (
      <TextField {...params} label="Branch *" placeholder="Code" InputLabelProps={{ shrink: true }} />
    )}
  />
  {/* NEW: Display Description after selection */}
  {row.branch && (
    <Typography variant="caption" sx={{ display: 'block', mt: 0.5, color: 'text.secondary', fontWeight: 500 }}>
      {row.branch.name}
    </Typography>
  )}
</Grid>

```
### 2. Update CGL Autocomplete (with MEMO handling)
We will add a similar description display and restore the "MEMO" chip logic, but make it smaller as requested.
**Replace your existing CGL Autocomplete (around line 718) with this:**
```javascript
{/* CGL */}
<Grid item size={{ xl: 2.3, lg: 3.1 }}>
  <Autocomplete
    autoHighlight
    popupIcon={null}
    options={cglSearch.options}
    getOptionLabel={(option) => option?.cglNumber ? String(option.cglNumber) : ""}
    renderOption={(props, option) => (
      <li {...props} key={option.cglNumber}>
        <Typography noWrap>{option.cglNumber} - {option.description}</Typography>
      </li>
    )}
    value={row.cgl || null}
    onChange={(event, newValue) => handleRowChange(row.id, "cgl", newValue)}
    inputValue={cglInputValues[row.id] || ""}
    onInputChange={(event, newInputValue, reason) => {
        if (reason === 'input') {
            const cleanInput = newInputValue.replace(CGL_REPLACE_REGEX, "").substring(0, 10);
            setCglInputValues(prev => ({ ...prev, [row.id]: cleanInput }));
            searchCgls(cleanInput, row.id);
        } else {
            setCglInputValues(prev => ({ ...prev, [row.id]: newInputValue }));
        }
    }}
    renderInput={(params) => (
      <TextField {...params} label="CGL *" placeholder="CGL" InputLabelProps={{ shrink: true }} />
    )}
  />
  
  {/* NEW: Display Description + Small MEMO Chip */}
  {row.cgl && (
    <Box sx={{ display: 'flex', alignItems: 'center', gap: 1, mt: 0.5 }}>
      <Typography variant="caption" sx={{ color: 'text.secondary', fontWeight: 500 }}>
        {row.cgl.description}
      </Typography>
      {String(row.cgl.cglNumber).startsWith("5") && (
        <Chip label="MEMO" size="small" sx={{ height: 16, fontSize: '0.6rem', backgroundColor: 'warning.light', color: 'white' }} />
      )}
    </Box>
  )}
</Grid>

```
### Why this works:
 1. **Branch Description**: By checking {row.branch && ...}, the UI now displays the branch name immediately below the branch code field once a valid branch is selected.
 2. **CGL Description & Memo**: The logic now checks row.cgl to display the description and conditionally renders the "MEMO" chip only if the CGL starts with "5".
 3. **UI Cleanliness**: The "MEMO" chip is now styled with height: 16 and fontSize: '0.6rem', which is significantly smaller and less intrusive than the standard chip size.
This will provide the user with clear feedback about what they have selected without cluttering the input fields themselves.
